{\"id\":\"node1-restart-recovery\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol: node1 healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id c8eba47a... with CA trust confirmed; systemd-run reboot acknowledged; node briefly unreachable; boot_id changed to 720679e7... (also matches fixed after_executor snapshot); post-restart CA trust and cross-node HTTPS verify (HTTP 200, Verify return code 0) confirmed recovery.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d52cda026607474a\",\"cmd-46e901172b5b425e\",\"cmd-1b971dacbcf8466b\",\"cmd-a027523b410e4791\",\"cmd-af8c7fcbd712414f\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},{\"id\":\"node2-restart-recovery\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol: node2 healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id 151c4aa9... with fleet-tls.service active/listening confirmed; systemd-run reboot acknowledged; boot_id changed to 49ce85f3... (matches fixed after_executor snapshot); post-restart shows service active/enabled, listening on 8443, CA still a trust anchor, and TLS verify OK both locally and from node1.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-12192afd96ae46af\",\"cmd-36a5cae9ac2c423e\",\"cmd-9fa502b7e3ee4342\",\"cmd-b8740cebcfb54461\",\"cmd-6d96480994df4305\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.78,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map directly to the task or the restart protocol: CA/key/cert generation, trust-store installation, the fleet-tls.service unit, and the firewalld port opening are all necessary and retained appropriately. One iteration was needed to get the service unit right: cmd-a8b208c44d5f46aa (return_code 2, stopped before enabling the service due to a clock-skew 'not yet valid' cert error) and cmd-715322946e7749b5 (created a first fleet-tls.service using '-naccept -1', which crash-looped per cmd-07307e84707544a3) were both corrected by cmd-fe23e44340404b15, which produced the final healthy unit — this is normal corrective iteration rather than an unrelated experiment, so it is not weighted heavily. Transient reboot timer/service units were explicitly cleaned up on both nodes (cmd-6d96480994df4305, cmd-b4818998267d4e7d). The main residue is uncleaned, non-sensitive scratch files left in /tmp on both nodes (node1: ca_ext.cnf, ca_gen_err.log, node2.crt, node2.csr, node2_ext.cnf; node2: node2.csr, node2_csr.cnf), visible in the after_executor temporary-files diff — none contain private key material, but they were never removed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a8b208c44d5f46aa\",\"cmd-715322946e7749b5\",\"cmd-07307e84707544a3\",\"cmd-fe23e44340404b15\",\"cmd-6d96480994df4305\",\"cmd-b4818998267d4e7d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task were satisfied: a self-signed 'Fleet Internal CA' was created on node1, a CA-signed TLS certificate for node2 (with correct SAN) was issued, node2 was configured to serve HTTPS on 8443 with that certificate (via an openssl s_server systemd unit, a reasonable substitute given no package repos were available), and the CA was installed as a trusted system anchor on both nodes — all verified end-to-end with curl and openssl s_client showing 'Verify return code: 0'. The independently required restart-evidence protocol was also completed correctly for both nodes, with distinct pre/post boot IDs and full post-restart recovery of trust and service state, corroborated by the fixed before/after snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"after_prepare is an alias of before_prepare (prepare was disabled), so preparer-vs-executor state could not be separately distinguished; all changes seen are attributable to the executor.\",\"Non-sensitive scratch files (CSR/cert/config fragments) were left in /tmp on both nodes and never cleaned 